## ===========================================================================
## THE MONKEY AND THE NECKLACE — the chain's first link
##
## The forest monkey and the stolen necklace (`story.ni:2695-2707`, the gems
## stall commotion; `story.ni:3840-3920`, monkey business at the Top of the
## Post). The chain it starts — banana → monkey → necklace → Teisha — is the
## change document's puzzle spine for the escape.
##
## ARMING (change document, "The monkey"): the monkey is off-stage until the
## commotion fires, and the 2009 first-entry-to-the-gems-stall trigger must
## not fire during the calm walk. The change document's wording ("onto the
## start of the chase") predates the same-day three-state split; arming at
## the literal chase (TE20) would deadlock the chain that produces TE20, so
## this file arms it at `hunted` — and keeps the gems stall as the STAGE,
## because Gentry's commotion text is written from inside that stall ("from
## the back of the stall"). So: first time Jack stands in the Exotic Gems
## Stall while hunted, the commotion fires and the monkey takes the post.
## (The arming clause lives on the Exotic Gems Stall's own create block in
## `grubbers-market.chord` — an offstage entity's every-turn clauses never
## run, so the stage owns the trigger.)
## BOTH readings (hunted-not-chase; location kept as staging) CONFIRMED by
## David, 2026-08-24 (change document, "The monkey", dated confirmation).
##
## HELD, with the reason:
##   * The monkey-escape beat (`story.ni:3873`, grab the necklace → the
##     monkey slides away down the wire and comes back later): a refusal
##     cannot carry mutations in Chord, so the flee-and-return loop has no
##     clean home; text and mutation are held together rather than printing
##     a departure that does not happen. The four random guard arms carry
##     the refusals meanwhile.
##   * The monkey "conversation" (`MONKEY_QUIP1-3` gibberish machinery,
##     `story.ni:3917+`): quip trees are rewritten, not ported, and this one
##     is generated-text machinery besides. Talking to the monkey falls to
##     the stdlib default for now.
##   * Kissing (`story.ni:3852`, "Are you crazy?…") — the platform parses no
##     kiss verb, so the clause can never fire; held with the line.
##   * Throwing-at and the source's shake/turn refusals; the initial-
##     appearance line ("Balancing on the southeast cable…") — no authored
##     initial-appearance surface; the description carries the cable line.
##   * The flash-loot show/give-to-others rules travel with TE20.
##
## Both held placeholders are resolved (David, 2026-08-24): the banana's
## quiet-lift is RETIRED (no calm lift — the stallkeeper blocks the bin; see
## `grubbers-market.chord`), and the necklace wear-refusal is carried
## verbatim (ruling at the phrase below).
##
## Every finished line is Gentry's, `story.ni:2705`/`:3846-3906` verbatim.
##
## Divergence: no has-negation surface in `while` conditions, so the plain
## idle pool is not suppressed while Jack holds the banana — the two idle
## clauses roll independently and can very occasionally both print where the
## source swapped pools outright.
## ===========================================================================

create the monkey
  a person
  aka forest monkey, little monkey, pet, rodent, animal
  pronouns it
  states: hidden, loose
  carries the necklace

  The monkey has silvery fur and black mask-like markings on its startlingly
  human face. It seems to regard you with a mixture of curiosity and
  amusement — as though you were some strange little animal whose antics it
  found entertaining. It grips the necklace protectively, tight against its
  tiny chest. The monkey is standing on the southeast cable.

  on every turn while the monkey is here and the player has the banana and one chance in 5
    phrase monkey-idle-banana
  end on

  on every turn while the monkey is here and one chance in 5
    phrase monkey-idle
  end on

  on the player listening
    refuse monkey-chatter
  end on

  on the player smelling
    refuse monkey-no-smell
  end on

  on the player taking
    refuse monkey-jumps-away
  end on

  on the player attacking
    refuse monkey-jumps-away
  end on

  on the player pushing
    refuse monkey-jumps-away
  end on

  on the player pulling
    refuse monkey-jumps-away
  end on

  on the player giving
    phrase monkey-trade when the monkey has the banana
    move the necklace to the player when the monkey has the banana
    move the banana offstage when the monkey has the banana
    move the monkey offstage when the player has the necklace
  end on

create the necklace
  aka gem, jewel, silver chain, starburst, star

  It's a beautiful gem, clear blue with a deep indigo star in its center,
  set in a silver-wrought starburst on a silver chain. It's easily worth the
  price of any piece of merchandise in the market.

  on the player taking while the monkey has the necklace
    refuse monkey-guards-necklace
  end on

  on the player wearing
    refuse necklace-not-worn
  end on

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE LINES — Gentry's, except the marked placeholder
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define phrase monkey-commotion
  Suddenly you hear a commotion from the back of the stall: the crash of a
  display case hitting the ground; shouts and curses from the stallkeeper;
  and a horrific, gibbering shriek.

  'Come back here, you flea-bitten thief!' yells the stallkeeper, and for a
  panicked moment you think he means *you*. Then you see something small and
  gray dart up to the roof of the stall...it's just a forest monkey, probably
  someone's pet running loose. And it's clutching one of the stallkeeper's
  necklaces in its tiny black fist.

  The monkey turns and screeches back at the stallkeeper, dodges a clumsily
  thrown dirt clod, and scampers away.
end phrase

define phrase monkey-trade
  You hold out the banana and wiggle it enticingly. 'Here, monkey. C'mon,
  little monkey,' you whisper, trying to ignore how stupid it sounds.

  The monkey creeps closer, eyes locked on the yellow fruit. It's only about
  a foot away from your out-stretched hand now. Its little face contorts
  into bizarre expressions as it wrestles with the conflicting desires to
  grab the tasty treat, hold onto the shiny trinket, and stay out of your
  reach, all at the same time.

  Suddenly the monkey lunges. You make a grab with your free hand. The
  monkey grabs the banana just as your fingers hook around the necklace's
  chain. The monkey screams, jumps six feet straight up, lands on your head,
  and scampers off down the wire behind you. Your heart leaps into your
  throat as you lose your balance on top of the pole. You totter, pinwheel
  your arms... and stay on.

  You're breathing hard, but you're safe. And you have the necklace.

  Somewhere far below you, a stallkeeper is cursing and wiping smushed
  banana off his face.
end phrase

define phrase monkey-guards-necklace, randomly
  The monkey screeches at you and backs away.
or
  The monkey jumps back, chattering angrily and holding the necklace out of
  reach.
or
  The monkey scampers back, clutching the necklace to its chest.
or
  The monkey hops back a few feet on the wire and blows a raspberry at you,
  as though daring you to come after it.
end phrase

define phrase monkey-idle, randomly
  The monkey bounces up and down on its wire, screeching happily.
or
  The monkey turns the necklace over and over in its tiny hands, staring
  intently into the gem's blue depths.
or
  The monkey tosses the necklace high in the air, swings itself under the
  wire and back up again with its tail, and at the last second catches the
  necklace in its little black hands.
or
  The monkey hoots at you.
or
  The monkey sniffs at you and shrieks with amusement.
end phrase

define phrase monkey-idle-banana, randomly
  The monkey sniffs at you excitedly, and emits a hopeful chirp.
or
  The monkey hops up and down on the wire, and makes a gesture with its tiny
  hands as though it were peeling a banana.
or
  The monkey stares hungrily at your banana.
end phrase

define phrase monkey-chatter
  The monkey chatters and hoots to itself constantly.
end phrase

define phrase monkey-no-smell
  It won't let you close enough to smell it, but hey, it's a monkey — it
  probably doesn't smell like roses.
end phrase

define phrase monkey-jumps-away
  The monkey screams and jumps away from you, chattering with rage and
  shaking its tiny fist.
end phrase

## Carried verbatim — RULED (David, 2026-08-24): "keep it verbatim, it lands
## differently now and that's the point." Gentry's disguise logic
## (`story.ni:3852`) read on the remake's frame is the remake's own line.

define phrase necklace-not-worn
  On second thought, better not. You're still trying to look like a boy, and
  boys just don't wear this kind of jewelry.
end phrase
